Keep a sensitive copy from stdin in wl-copy's own memory.

Stock wl-copy has a forked cat write what it reads on stdin to
$TMPDIR/wl-copy-buffer-XXXXXX/stdin, unlinks it once it holds it open,
and serves every paste by running "cat" found on PATH on that file. Any
process of the user's can open the file before it's unlinked and read a
password copied with --sensitive, and a cat planted first on PATH is
handed it at each paste.

With --sensitive, this reads stdin to its end into anonymous pages that
are locked out of swap and left out of core dumps, growing them up to a
cap of 1 MiB, and serves each paste from them with write(2), running
nothing. It refuses the copy, exiting 1, when stdin can't be read whole
or the pages can't be locked, never falling back to a temp file. It makes
itself undumpable before reading, so the user's other processes can't
read it through /proc or ptrace, lets go of stdin once read, takes
text/plain;charset=utf-8 as the type rather than asking xdg-mime, and
wipes the pages on every exit that runs code: a cancel, a replacing copy,
SIGTERM and SIGINT. A SIGKILL leaves them to the kernel, which clears
them before any other process is given them. Copies from argv, and those
without --sensitive, are as before.

With --say-held as well, it says "ready" on stdout once it's undumpable,
before reading stdin, and "held" once the compositor has its offer, then
lets go of stdout. So a caller writes the secret only once nothing else
of the user's can read it, and can tell a copy refused from one held.

